# Development Skill Instructions

Use this file as the project entrypoint before starting any development task.

## Required Reading

Read these files before planning or coding:

- `docs/01-requirements-template.md` - user requirement intake template.
- `docs/02-development-workflow.md` - staged development workflow and planning rules.
- `docs/03-technical-standards.md` - engineering, testing, and delivery standards.
- `docs/04-design-standards.md` - UI, visual design, color, and content standards.
- `docs/05-execution-checklist.md` - per-task execution checklist.
- `docs/06-development-log-standard.md` - daily development log format and update rules.
- `docs/08-github-update-standard.md` - GitHub update and delivery rules after code changes.
- `docs/09-quality-gates-and-review-loop.md` - deterministic constraints, automated review loops, and generation/evaluation separation.
- `docs/10-session-checkpoints-and-recovery.md` - stateless session startup, progress tracking, Git checkpoints, and recovery rules.
- `docs/11-execution-tracking-quality-feedback.md` - execution tracking, quality grading, anomaly detection, and feedback attribution.
- `docs/12-ecc-selective-migration-policy.md` - ECC module migration boundaries, command recipes, hook policy, orchestration policy, and conditional module activation.

For any page, frontend, web app, website, dashboard, game, or interactive UI development, also use the installed `frontend-design` skill at:

- `C:\Users\54711\.codex\skills\frontend-design\SKILL.md`

For high-quality landing pages, portfolios, and redesigns, use the installed `taste-skill` for aesthetic direction:

- `C:\Users\54711\.codex\skills\taste-skill\SKILL.md`
- Skill install name: `design-taste-frontend`

Use `graphify` as an auxiliary development tool for architecture and codebase understanding:

- Skill path: `C:\Users\54711\.codex\skills\graphify\SKILL.md`
- CLI: `graphify` or `C:\Users\54711\.local\bin\graphify.exe`
- Reference repo clone: `work\graphify`

Before answering architecture, module relationship, file relationship, impact analysis, or large codebase questions, prefer `graphify` when `graphify-out/graph.json` exists. When no graph exists, create one only when the task benefits from structural codebase analysis.

Use GitHub for code update delivery:

- GitHub skill path: `C:\Users\54711\.codex\skills\github\SKILL.md`
- GitHub CLI: `gh`
- After code is modified and verified, update through GitHub by committing and pushing, or by preparing a branch and pull request when that is the project policy.
- If the project has no Git repository or GitHub remote, record that as a blocker or setup todo instead of pretending the update was published.

## Core Workflow

1. Start every session as stateless.
   - Rebuild context from `AGENT.nd`, `docs/`, today's `dev-logs/YYYY-MM-DD.md`, Git status, and existing TODO/checklists.
   - Do not assume chat history is complete.
   - Identify the last completed checkpoint and the next unfinished task before editing.

2. Start with requirements.
   - Ask the user to complete the requirement template before implementation.
   - If any field is missing, make a conservative assumption and write it down before coding.
   - Do not build from vague intent when the target device, main problem, core functions, or visual direction is unknown.

3. Plan in stable increments.
   - Break work into small phases that can be implemented, tested, and reviewed independently.
   - Avoid doing too much at once. Prefer a reliable MVP, then add the next feature.
   - Each phase must define scope, expected result, validation method, and next step.
   - Maintain a feature checklist and TODO list for active work.
   - For complex features, architectural changes, or broad refactors, use a planning role before editing: clarify requirements, identify affected files, list risks, define phases, and define verification.

4. Use frontend-design by default for page development.
   - Before planning or implementing UI, read and apply `frontend-design`.
   - Treat it as the default design and UI QA standard for frontend work.
   - Confirm product type, target user, primary workflow, design style, colors, and displayed content before building.
   - When using `taste-skill` and `Ilm-Alan/frontend-design` together, use `taste-skill` for aesthetic direction and `Ilm-Alan/frontend-design` only to convert that direction into concrete CSS tokens.
   - If the two conflict, preserve the `taste-skill` design direction and resolve implementation details through `Ilm-Alan/frontend-design` tokens.

5. Use graphify for auxiliary codebase analysis.
   - Use `graphify query`, `graphify path`, or `graphify explain` when a graph already exists.
   - Use `/graphify .` or `graphify extract .` only when building a graph is worth the time and scope.
   - After modifying code files in a project that has `graphify-out/graph.json`, run `graphify update .` when practical.
   - Avoid scanning generated folders, dependency folders, or unrelated cloned tools unless they are the actual analysis target.

6. Keep project records current.
   - Use `dev-logs/YYYY-MM-DD.md` for daily records.
   - At the start of a work session, create today's log if it does not exist.
   - At the end of a work session, append completed work, decisions, open issues, and next todos.

7. Keep docs synchronized.
   - Put project requirements, technical choices, design rules, implementation steps, and acceptance criteria in `docs/`.
   - Update docs when decisions change.
   - Do not leave important project knowledge only in chat history.

8. Convert repeated Agent mistakes into rules.
   - When the Agent makes the same type of mistake more than once, add a concise prevention rule to `AGENT.nd`.
   - The rule must describe the trigger, the required behavior, and the forbidden behavior.
   - Keep the rule actionable; do not add vague reminders.
   - Record the added rule and reason in `dev-logs/YYYY-MM-DD.md`.

9. Deliver safely.
   - Make focused edits.
   - Verify the changed behavior.
   - Report what changed, what was tested, and what remains pending.

10. Apply quality gates before delivery.
   - Use deterministic constraints first: linter, formatter, type checks, structural tests, and pre-commit where available.
   - Use an automated review loop for risky changes: implementation, review, fix, repeat until acceptance criteria are satisfied.
   - Separate generation and evaluation: the agent that creates the solution should not be the only evaluator of the result.
   - For substantial work, use independent review or evaluator agents when available.
   - For feature, bugfix, and refactor work, prefer TDD: define user journey, write test, confirm valid RED, implement minimal fix, confirm GREEN, then refactor.
   - Verification must report build, type check, lint, tests, security scan, and diff review when those checks exist.
   - For AI workflow or prompt changes, define capability and regression evals before implementation and track pass/fail results.
   - Treat repeated corrections and useful patterns as project-scoped learning candidates; promote only stable cross-project patterns to global rules.
   - Apply adopted ECC `rules-core` constraints: research and reuse before net-new implementation, explicit input validation, immutable updates for shared data, focused files/functions, conventional commits, security review triggers, and no untested delivery.
   - For TypeScript, React, or web frontend projects, enable the conditional frontend rules in `docs/03-technical-standards.md` and `docs/09-quality-gates-and-review-loop.md`; do not apply unrelated language rules before the stack is known.
   - Apply adopted ECC `agents-core` role triggers without installing all agent files: planning role for complex scope, TDD role for feature/bug/refactor work, code review role after code edits, security review role for sensitive boundaries, build resolver role after build/type failures, and stack-specific review roles only after the project stack is known.
   - Do not spawn or simulate extra review roles for trivial changes when deterministic checks and a local review pass are enough.
   - Apply adopted ECC `commands-core` recipes as workflow patterns, not slash-command files: plan, feature development, checkpoint, quality gate, security scan, build fix, session save/resume, and PR creation.
   - Apply adopted ECC `agentic-patterns` only when the task benefits from agentic execution: define completion criteria, split into independently verifiable units, track cost/retries, and use human-controlled merge gates.
   - Apply ECC `security` rules by default for sensitive changes; activate deeper security scan guidance when configuration, hooks, MCP, auth, secrets, external APIs, or dependency surfaces change.
   - Treat ECC `hooks-runtime`, `platform-configs`, `framework-language`, `database`, `orchestration`, `devops-infra`, `machine-learning`, media, business, operator, and domain modules as conditional. Activate them only when requirements or project stack justify them, and record the decision.
   - Never bulk copy ECC runtime files, commands, hooks, agents, platform configs, or scripts into a project without explicit approval and rollback.

11. Create Git checkpoints for code changes.
   - Commit incrementally after coherent, verified steps.
   - Treat each commit as a recovery snapshot.
   - Do not bundle unrelated work into one checkpoint.
   - If a failure occurs, inspect the latest checkpoint, current diff, and TODO list, then continue from the interrupted task.

12. Update through GitHub after code changes.
   - Check `git status` before and after edits.
   - Commit only relevant changed files.
   - Push to the configured GitHub remote or prepare a pull request.
   - Never force-push, rewrite history, merge, delete branches, or change repository settings unless explicitly requested.
   - If GitHub update cannot be completed, record the reason in `dev-logs/YYYY-MM-DD.md`.

13. Close with execution tracking and feedback attribution.
   - Record execution trace: task, phase, changed files, commands/checks, result, blockers, and next action.
   - Assign a quality grade before handoff: A, B, C, or Blocked.
   - Detect anomalies such as repeated failures, skipped checks, unclear requirements, unexpected diffs, test regressions, or tool timeouts.
   - Attribute feedback to the likely source: requirement gap, planning gap, implementation bug, design issue, verification gap, tooling issue, or Agent rule gap.
   - If feedback reveals a repeated Agent rule gap, update `AGENT.nd`.

